<worksheet>
  <title>LT 4 Slope and Y-intercept Discovery</title>
<p>
  Go to Desmos and type in the equations <m>y=mx</m> and <m>y=mx+b</m>.  Click on "all" sliders.
</p>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Let <m>m=1</m>.  Move the <m>b</m> slider, make observations, and answer the following questions.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task> 
    <statement>
      <p>
        What do you notice when <m>b>0</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The y-intercept is above the x-axis, and all the points move up b-units.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
  <task>  
    <statement>
      <p>
        What do you notice when <m>b=0</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The y-intercept is at the origin (0,0).
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
  <task> 
    <statement>
      <p>
        What do you notice when <m>b</m> is less than 0?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The y-intercept is below the x-axis, and all the points move down b-units.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
</exercise>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Let <m>b=1</m>.  Move the <m>m</m> slider, make observations, and answer the following questions.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace="0.0001in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What do you notice when <m>m>0</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The line goes up from left to right; it increases.  The bigger the value for <m>m</m>, the steeper the line.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What do you notice when <m>m=0</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The line is horizontal.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What do you notice when <m>m</m> is less than 0?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The line goes down from left to right; it decreases.  The smaller the <m>b</m> value is, the steeper the line becomes. 
      </p> 
    </answer>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What do you notice when the slope is close to zero on the right side?.....left side?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        When slope is very close to zero, on either side, the line is very flat.  This is because the when the <m>m=0</m>, the line is horizontal.  If the slope is not much bigger than 0, the line will not be much steeper than horizontal. 
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What do you notice when the slope is farther from zero on the right side (very large)...the left side (very small)?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The farther the slope gets from zero, the steeper the line gets.  The line is getting further away from being horizontal.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
</exercise> 
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Consider the next several questions.  Each equation contains a three (3).  Discuss with your group ho the 3 of the equation will affect the graph of <m>y=x</m>.  Do not graph before making your decision.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      <m>y=x+3</m>
    </p>
    <p>
      The slope changes from 1 to 3 so the line is steeper. The output values will be 3 times what they were before. The x and y intercepts stay the same (0,0).
    </p>

    <p>The slope is the same; all output values are three less than they were on the line y = x. The graph will be 3 units down and parallel to the graph of y=x. [i.e.  (0,0) becomes (0, -3)]
    </p>

    <p>The slope is the same; all output values are three more than they were on the line y = x. The graph will be 3 units up and parallel to the graph of y=x. [i.e.  (0,0) becomes (0, 3)]
    </p>
    
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p>
      The slope is the same; all output values are three more than they were on the line y = x. The graph will be 3 units up and parallel to the graph of y=x. [i.e.  (0,0) becomes (0, 3)]
    </p>
  </answer>
  </task> 
   <task>
      <statement>
       <p>
           <m>y=x-3</m>
       </p>
        <p>
      The slope changes from 1 to 3 so the line is steeper. The output values will be 3 times what they were before. The x and y intercepts stay the same (0,0).
    </p>

    <p>The slope is the same; all output values are three less than they were on the line y = x. The graph will be 3 units down and parallel to the graph of y=x. [i.e.  (0,0) becomes (0, -3)]
    </p>

    <p>The slope is the same; all output values are three more than they were on the line y = x. The graph will be 3 units up and parallel to the graph of y=x. [i.e.  (0,0) becomes (0, 3)]
    </p>
     </statement>
     <answer>
      <p>
        The slope is the same; all output values are three less than they were on the line y = x. The graph will be 3 units down and parallel to the graph of y=x. [i.e.  (0,0) becomes (0, -3)]
      </p>
     </answer>
   </task>
   <task>
      <statement>
       <p>
           <m>y=3x</m>
       </p>
        <p>
      The slope changes from 1 to 3 so the line is steeper. The output values will be 3 times what they were before. The x and y intercepts stay the same (0,0).
    </p>

    <p>The slope is the same; all output values are three less than they were on the line y = x. The graph will be 3 units down and parallel to the graph of y=x. [i.e.  (0,0) becomes (0, -3)]
    </p>

    <p>The slope is the same; all output values are three more than they were on the line y = x. The graph will be 3 units up and parallel to the graph of y=x. [i.e.  (0,0) becomes (0, 3)]
    </p>
     </statement>
     <answer>
      <p>
        The slope changes from 1 to 3 so the line is steeper. The output values will be 3 times what they were before. The x and y intercepts stay the same (0,0).
      </p>
     </answer>
   </task>
   
 
  </exercise>
</worksheet>







